before i was at tasty i was living and  working in stockholm sweden and i always  used to joke that the swedes had a bun  for every single season and the bun for  the winter season is luciboular hey hey  i'm alex i'm a tasty producer and today  i'm going to show you guys how to make  lucilla or saffron buns lucivilla are so  beautiful but don't let that intimidate  you in making them they're actually  pretty simple now some people might  grind up their saffron threads into a  fine powder however i found that once  you're done kneading the dough the  saffron threads have pretty much  dissolved into it so i just throw them  right in saffron buns are rich and  buttery like a brioche they have a  little bit of sweetness a little bit of  spice and of course a vibrant yellow  color from the saffron infused dough  cardamom isn't traditionally put in most  saffron buns but i like to add a little  bit just to give it an extra punch in  flavor  flavor punch  i found it easier to make a well and put  the wet ingredients into that well and  slowly start incorporating the flour  into the wet that way and that will help  you to incorporate the ingredients  completely and not end up with a lumpy  dough because no one wants a lumpy dough  even in the dark in the cold christmas  time in sweden was my absolute favorite  time of year there's christmas markets  pretty lights everywhere snow and of  course saffron buttons unfortunately  they're only really available in  december but in learning how to make  them you could eat them all year long  kneading the dough is a super important  step in order to develop the gluten and  get a really light and fluffy bun you  can find these buns in december in  bakeries across scandinavia but they're  actually meant to be eaten on december  13th which is santa lucia day the  festival of light and it's meant to  bring light and warmth to the coldest  month of the year  punching down the dough will help  release some of those gas bubbles and  redistribute the use of the dough first  i pull off some of the dough and form a  ball the ball should be about two inches  in width and then i start rolling out  that ball into a long rope if you want  super even sized buns you can measure  your dough throughout this shaping  process but i usually just eyeball it  traditionally they're shaped in an s  shape meant to look like a curled up cat  and that's why they're also sometimes  called lucicator  proofing is the final rise of the shaped  bread dough before baking and it's an  important step so don't skip it the egg  wash will help give a really beautiful  golden brown and shiny finish to the  buns while baking  i miss sweden so much but making these  buns it's like a little piece of sweden  right here in california so  traditionally the buns have two raisins  that are placed in the center of each  spiral and these are meant to look like  the eyes of the cat which i don't know  if i particularly see but hey can't  complain once these buns go into the  oven  buns in the oven  10 to 12 minutes and you'll have a  delicious lucibola ready to eat when i'm  traveling my absolute favorite way to  absorb another culture is through the  food but if you don't have the  opportunity to travel learning how to  make other cultures dishes at home is a  great way to explore  brings me right back now those are some  good looking buns  oh yes  [Music]  you
 

